WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.140
China is currently experiencing the
largest mass migration ever witnessed in

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:13.259
human history. Over the past 30 years,
China's urban population has grown by

00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:17.340
over half a billion people, thanks
largely to a vast exodus of peasants

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.760
from the countryside. And the Chinese
government is trying to speed this

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.900
influx through an official urbanization
drive, so as to bolster the country's

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.250
declining economic growth rate. In theory,
urbanization makes a lot of economic

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:33.510
sense: Urban workers tend to be more
productive. But in China, the domestic

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:37.500
migration comes with a big political
catch: the catch known as the hukou

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.489
system, an identification and
registration program utilized for social

00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:45.870
control by the Ministry of Public
Security. In practice, most peasants who

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:50.070
move to urban areas for better jobs
keep their rural hukou, meaning they cannot

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:54.690
obtain urban social services, can't bring
their children along, earn less than

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:59.670
comparably skilled urban counterparts,
and face further discrimination, too. Sounds

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:03.960
almost like a apartheid-era South
Africa. Can such a repressive arrangement

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.860
really last forever?

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.700
What do you think is
China's most pressing population problem?

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.860
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know
what other topics you'd like our

